{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Establish a usable integrity baseline covering /etc on node1.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A root-protected SHA-256 manifest was initialized with 1,318 entries, and the final check directly showed that /etc matched the baseline. Four boot/runtime-derived files were deliberately excluded; this is a reasonable noise-control choice rather than evidence that general /etc coverage is absent.","evidence_ids":["cmd-1ff240fa89a34c8d","cmd-26a56abd241c4eb6"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Allow later changes under /etc to be identified and reported.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Controlled additions under /etc produced path, metadata, and SHA-256 details with alert status 1. A reboot also directly produced CHANGED reports for modified files. Reports were persisted, both probes were removed, and the final integrity check was clean. A protected daily cron hook provides ongoing execution.","evidence_ids":["cmd-86658c6f95544b8f","cmd-0d3886974d934362","cmd-1d61913283de4324","cmd-26a56abd241c4eb6"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The implementation successfully used the installed legacy coreutils and shell facilities. CentOS 5.11 and kernel 2.6.18-398.el5 were unchanged in final observations.","evidence_ids":["cmd-b67487d5118a44a1","cmd-a1b366c95cc642f5","cmd-26a56abd241c4eb6","global:before_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform"]}],"confidence":0.93,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.96,"summary":"The task mutations were generally necessary and controlled: installation and baseline creation supported the required outcome, integrity probes were bounded and removed, and the post-restart baseline refinement corrected observed runtime noise. One unnecessary low-risk mutation was identified: cmd-f4db52f8d8c0495c redirected reboot output to /var/tmp/etc-integrity-reboot.log, but that file was never used as evidence or explicitly cleaned. Whether it remained is indeterminate because the final temporary-file inventory did not cover /var/tmp. No new listening sockets, /tmp residue, platform change, or demonstrated package damage appeared in lifecycle observations.","evidence_ids":["cmd-a1b366c95cc642f5","cmd-86658c6f95544b8f","cmd-f4db52f8d8c0495c","cmd-1ff240fa89a34c8d","cmd-1d61913283de4324","cmd-26a56abd241c4eb6","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:platform"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct evidence: node1 has a protected /etc integrity baseline, controlled changes are detected and logged, daily checking is configured, and the legacy OS and kernel were preserved. The provider restart protocol demonstrated a real reboot and recovery, but it was not repeated after the final checker edit and baseline reinitialization.","limitations":["The mandatory restart occurred before cmd-1ff240fa89a34c8d modified the checker and reinitialized the final baseline. Thus the fixed boot identities and reboot evidence prove a genuine restart, but not persistence of the exact final implementation through a subsequent restart.","The four excluded runtime-derived files are not themselves monitored; evidence does not independently inspect every claimed authoritative source, although general /etc coverage and change detection are directly demonstrated.","The lifecycle package-health probe is unsupported in practice on this legacy yum version because it printed yum usage in both snapshots, so package consistency cannot be independently assessed.","Final lifecycle collection inventories /tmp but not /var/tmp, leaving the disposition of the reboot log created by cmd-f4db52f8d8c0495c uncertain."]}
